            "text": "At dawn, the city briefly belongs to small sounds. A delivery bicycle clicks over a loose drain cover; pigeons negotiate the roofline; somewhere, behind an apartment window, a kettle begins its low argument with the morning. The streets are not empty, exactly, but they have not yet remembered their urgency.\n\nI like this hour because ordinary things seem newly invented. Bread cooling on metal racks appears miraculous. A bus turning the corner carries three passengers and all their private futures. Even the traffic lights, changing for no one, look less like machines than patient guardians practicing an old ritual.\n\nSoon, shutters rise. Phones wake. Coffee crosses counters in paper cups, and people begin exchanging weather, apologies, passwords, and plans. The day gathers speed by pretending speed was always its nature. Yet beneath the appointments and notifications, the quieter world remains: dust revolving in sunlight, roots pressing through soil, water moving under bridges without needing praise.\n\nPerhaps attention is simply the art of refusing to let familiarity become invisibility. A chipped bowl can hold peaches like a painting. A neighbor\u2019s laugh through a wall can prove that solitude is not the same as abandonment. A worn coat can remember every winter it survived.\n\nBy noon, wonder usually hides beneath efficiency, but it does not disappear. It waits in overlooked places, ready to return when we pause: in the scent of rain on stone, the warmth left in a chair, or the sudden kindness of someone holding a door open behind us."
